<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acts of Thomas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Acts of Thomas is an early 3rd-century Christian text, probably written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> at Edessa, telling of the mission of the Apostle Thomas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It describes him being sold as a carpenter to an envoy of King Gudnaphar, a name that matches the Indo-Parthian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondophares">Gondophares</a>, known from coins and inscriptions. It is the basis of the tradition of Thomas as the apostle of India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acts-of-thomas.jpg" alt="Acts of Thomas" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WolfgangRieger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Tchacos_p33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
